# ==========================================
# Admin-portal settings for the Docker dev stack
# ==========================================
#
# Setup
#   1. cp .env.docker.example .env.docker   (gitignored; never commit it)
#   2. Fill in every value under "1. Required". Everything after that is optional.
#   3. cd apps/admin-portal
#      export COMPOSE_ENV_FILES=../../.env.docker
#      docker compose up -d
#   4. Create the admin account (section 3)
#
# Who reads this file
#   - Docker Compose itself, for the POSTGRES_* and REDIS_PASSWORD values that
#     set up the database and Redis containers.
#   - The app container, which gets every line here. Exceptions:
#     docker-compose.yml sets DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL and OLLAMA_URL to the
#     container hostnames, and sets OPENAI_API_KEY from
#     ADMIN_PORTAL_OPENAI_API_KEY. Those win over anything written here.
#   - Nothing outside Docker. `pnpm nx run admin-portal:dev:lite` on the host
#     reads its settings from the shell (see CLAUDE.md).
#   - The website needs nothing from this file.
#
# Every setting the app reads is listed here (apart from NODE_ENV and ones set
# by test and build tooling), so anything else in your .env.docker is unused.

# ==========================================
# 1. Required
# ==========================================
# Database and Redis passwords for the dev containers. Any strong values.
# Postgres only takes its password when its volume is first created, so
# changing it later means changing it in the database too.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=your_secure_password_here
REDIS_PASSWORD=your_redis_password_here
# Optional; these are the defaults
# POSTGRES_USER=business_portal
# POSTGRES_DB=business_portal

# Signs login sessions. At least 32 characters: `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# Without it, sign-in is skipped in development and every request is refused
# in production.
AUTH_SECRET=your_auth_secret_at_least_32_chars

# ==========================================
# 2. AI
# ==========================================
# OpenAI key for development. The app receives it as OPENAI_API_KEY. Needed
# whatever AI_PROVIDER is: embeddings always use OpenAI, so without it uploads
# fail at the embedding step and search returns nothing.
ADMIN_PORTAL_OPENAI_API_KEY=your_admin_portal_openai_api_key

# The chat model. AI_MODEL must belong to AI_PROVIDER:
#   openai    → gpt-*      (key: ADMIN_PORTAL_OPENAI_API_KEY)
#   anthropic → claude-*   (key: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)
#   gemini    → gemini-*   (key: GOOGLE_API_KEY)
#   ollama / ollama-remote → a local model (no key; see section 7)
# Unset, it defaults to anthropic + claude-sonnet-4-6. The app checks this at
# start-up: a warning in development, fatal in production and demo mode.
# gpt-4.1-nano is cheap and is the demo's model, so dev matches the demo.
AI_PROVIDER=openai
AI_MODEL=gpt-4.1-nano

# Only for the matching AI_PROVIDER above
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=                # also used by Google Places (section 6)

# Cost controls (optional outside demo mode, where the daily caps are off)
# AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=4096      # cap on every call's output
# AI_DAILY_BUDGET_USD=           # total spend per Brisbane day; demo default 2
# AI_USER_DAILY_BUDGET_USD=      # per user per day; demo default 0.25
# AI_USER_REQUESTS=30            # AI requests per user per window
# AI_USER_WINDOW_MINUTES=10

# Check the AI setup with one paid call (about US$0.0002):
#   docker compose exec app pnpm --filter admin-portal ai:smoke

# ==========================================
# 3. Admin account
# ==========================================
# There is no default admin, and signup creates members. Create the admin once:
#   docker compose exec -w /app/apps/admin-portal app pnpm db:seed-admin
# ADMIN_EMAIL is required for that. Without ADMIN_PASSWORD a random password is
# generated and printed once.
# ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# ADMIN_NAME=Admin

# ==========================================
# 4. Demo mode (leave off for development)
# ==========================================
# DEMO_MODE=true switches off the features listed in lib/demo.ts and makes
# Cloudflare Access the only way in. It needs AI_PROVIDER=openai and an
# AI_MODEL with a price in lib/ai/pricing.ts (gpt-4.1-nano has one).
# DEMO_MODE=true

# From the Cloudflare Access application in front of the demo (set up with the
# Cloud Run deployment):
# CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=https://<team>.cloudflareaccess.com
# CF_ACCESS_AUD=<application AUD tag>

# To try demo mode locally without Access: sign in as this email instead.
# Refused when NODE_ENV=production.
# DEV_ACCESS_IDENTITY=you@example.com

# The demo's own OpenAI key, kept apart from your development key. Create it
# in a separate OpenAI project:
#   1. platform.openai.com → Projects → Create project (e.g. "admin-portal demo")
#   2. In that project: API keys → Create new secret key
#   3. Project settings → Limits → Edit spend limit: set a monthly amount and
#      turn on "Enforce a hard limit". Requests over it fail with 429
#      project_spend_limit_exceeded. Spend can overshoot slightly, so add alerts.
#      The app's own caps reset when the server restarts, so this limit is the
#      real cap: keep it below about $60 a month (the $2 daily cap × 30), or
#      lower AI_DAILY_BUDGET_USD.
# Nothing reads this yet: the demo container run (coming with the demo seed)
# passes it to the app as OPENAI_API_KEY, and on Cloud Run it comes from
# Secret Manager instead.
# DEMO_OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ==========================================
# 5. Database, queue and rate-limit drivers
# ==========================================
# The defaults are this Docker setup. The single-instance demo uses
# pglite + memory: no Postgres or Redis at all.
# DB_DRIVER=postgres          # postgres | pglite
# QUEUE_DRIVER=bullmq         # bullmq | memory
# RATE_LIMIT_DRIVER=memory    # memory | redis (redis needs QUEUE_DRIVER=bullmq)
# PGLITE_DATA_DIR=            # pglite only; unset means in memory
# PGLITE_SNAPSHOT=            # pglite only; dumpDataDir() tarball to seed a new database
# UPLOADS_DIR=                # uploaded files; default ./uploads in the app's working directory

# ==========================================
# 6. Optional features (each is off or degraded without its key)
# ==========================================
# Client lookup: ABN Lookup GUID (abr.business.gov.au) and Brave Search
# (brave.com/search/api). Google Places uses GOOGLE_API_KEY from section 2.
# ABN_LOOKUP_GUID=
# BRAVE_SEARCH_API=
# Research agents' news tool (newsapi.org)
# NEWSAPI_KEY=
# Content audit: where to fetch the website's blog posts from
# BUSINESS_PORTAL_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ==========================================
# 7. Development conveniences
# ==========================================
# Ollama on another machine (AI_PROVIDER=ollama-remote). Local Ollama
# (`docker compose --profile ai up -d`) needs nothing here.
# OLLAMA_REMOTE_URL=http://your_remote_host:11434
# LAN hosts (IP or hostname) allowed to load the dev server, comma-separated
# ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS=192.168.1.20,192.168.1.21
# Set to false to turn sign-in off entirely (never with DEMO_MODE)
# AUTH_ENABLED=true
